WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.760
- Have a seat.
(audience clapping)

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:02.810
I like your blue.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:03.830
I like your whole outfit.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:04.663
- Thank you.

00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:05.810
I didn't make it myself.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:06.870
- But you're wearing it well.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:07.960
- Thank you very much.
- Yes.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:08.930
- This is so funny.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.670
There's so many musical
talented people out here

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.950
and I don't know how to-

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.240
- Do you sing?

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.328
Want me to put you on the spot?

00:00:16.328 --> 00:00:17.304
I'm kidding. (laughs)
- You know how like you...

00:00:17.304 --> 00:00:18.137
(audience laughs)
Can you sing right now?

00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:20.210
You know how like you grow up in a family

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.210
where everybody sings
and you're around people

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.197
who sing all the time-
- Yeah.

00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:25.290
- And then it's inherited?
That didn't happen to me.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.178
- Oh.
(all laughing)

00:00:26.178 --> 00:00:27.213
I am the opposite of that. (laughs)

00:00:27.213 --> 00:00:28.046
- Are you tone deaf?

00:00:28.046 --> 00:00:30.317
Or you just like don't sing?
- I can go-

00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:31.150
- Can you you go, "Aah..."?

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.088
Can you match that? Aah..
- No. No.

00:00:32.088 --> 00:00:33.046
Aah...

00:00:33.046 --> 00:00:36.160
(all laughing)

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.343
I made the noise.

00:00:37.343 --> 00:00:39.610
- He said, "Aah..." (laughs)

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.230
- By the way, you're so
good in "Uncut Gems".

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.175
- Oh, thanks! That's not-
- I loved you in it.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:44.160
(audience claps)
- It's such a musical thing,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.397
but she's so good in it.

00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:46.230
- [Kelly] It is. I know.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.063
- I know, sorry. Go ahead.

00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:48.070
- No, you go ahead.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:48.940
- The first-
- I talk a lot.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.410
- So the first job I ever did as an actor

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.510
was in "Bye Bye Birdie", the musical,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.125
you know, "Bye Bye Birdie"?
- Yeah.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:55.958
(audience clapping)

00:00:55.958 --> 00:00:56.791
What'd you sing in that?

00:00:56.791 --> 00:00:57.624
- I dunno.
- Have you seen that yet?

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:58.460
- I was in the chorus.
(all laughing)

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.620
There was only like four guys
in the chorus when I did it.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.453
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:06.390
- And you know, you had to
do rehearsal and everything,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.230
and I didn't know how to
sing, so I used to lip sync.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.000
I used to just do, I'd be like...

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.110
I'd mouth it
(all laughing)

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.520
because if anyone heard it, as soon as...

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.970
And then I'd be like, maybe I can sing.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.370
Maybe I'll just give it a try,
and I'd be like going steady.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:17.203
And then right away,

00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:18.980
the vocal coach would be
like, "Oh, we're flat there".

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:19.813
Something-
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.540
- Something happened.
- Something happened.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.200
And I'd be like, oh, I dunno what to do.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.410
And then I'd just mouth it again,

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.243
and then as soon as I'd sing again

00:01:25.243 --> 00:01:27.838
she'd be like, "Ooh, we
got some work to do".

00:01:27.838 --> 00:01:28.671
(all laughing)
So that's-

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:29.950
- But she wouldn't say anything
when you were lip syncing

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.920
and nothing was going out? (laughing)

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.530
- Nothing. No, because they sounded good.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.560
I sounded so bad.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.393
- Oh, I see what you're saying.

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:36.156
- Right, whenever he joined
in, there was a problem.

00:01:36.156 --> 00:01:38.182
(all laughing)

00:01:38.182 --> 00:01:39.015
- I'm sorry, it's a little late.

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:39.848
- That's why I don't even
know how to put it into words

00:01:39.848 --> 00:01:42.890
how I can't sing, but I'm
in awe of all of you people.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:43.780
I don't know how you do it.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.920
- Well, I am an awe of you.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.000
I'm not supposed to do this,
but I'm jumping right to it

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.240
because I love the show so much.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.107
- Oh.
- I think it's so good.

00:01:49.107 --> 00:01:51.220
"61st Street" is the show.
- Yep.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.710
- And it's so important.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.300
It's such a great, important piece of work

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.540
I think, that's going on right now.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.420
Like, it's very intense.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.420
- It's very intense.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.370
I mean-
- 'Cause it's real.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:03.800
- Yeah. It's happening, you know?

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.030
- [Kelly] Yeah, like those kind
of situations are happening.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.600
- And I think (stammers)

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.100
we're so lucky to get to do this job.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.760
So it's like to do something
that actually matters.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.210
And I think sometimes with
the news and everything,

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.150
you kind of get desensitized to it.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.300
So when it's like a story you're watching,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.010
I think actually you start to
identify with it a bit more,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.000
and it shows the line between morality

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.670
and what's right and wrong,
on such a large scale.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:28.730
- And then there's like some obvious

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.600
just like way wrong. (laughs)

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.110
- Yeah. Like way, way, way wrong.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.910
- You're like, "How
did they miss the line?

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.000
It's not gray." (laughs)

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.130
- Exactly, and how did no one
notice that that was wrong?

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:37.963
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:37.963 --> 00:02:39.464
- And that's the thing
that really drew me to it,

00:02:39.464 --> 00:02:42.220
it's an important show
that I think you need to...

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.810
I mean, there's a line on the show

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.360
where Courtney B. Vance
says we need to learn

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.820
to walk in one another's shoes.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:47.653
- [Kelly] Mm-hmm.

00:02:47.653 --> 00:02:48.486
- I think that is so important.

00:02:48.486 --> 00:02:49.950
- Oh, 100%.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.530
Did you do like a ride-along

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.300
to get into the role or anything?

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.560
- I did do a ride-along and you know,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.620
I'm always nervous.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.140
I've done a few ride-alongs before

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.390
when I played police officers,
and I'm always like a bit...

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.680
You kind of want some action to happen,

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.250
but you really shouldn't
want that to happen.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.210
- Yeah, then you kinda feel like a jerk,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.598
'cause you're like-
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:06.598 --> 00:03:07.431
I'm like, "Maybe's this
one's gonna rob a bank".

00:03:07.431 --> 00:03:08.691
I'm like, why would I want that?

00:03:08.691 --> 00:03:09.524
(all laughing)

00:03:09.524 --> 00:03:10.660
I just wanna flick on the siren,

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.270
but every time I'm
around a police officer,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.520
I always feel like I did something wrong.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.650
You know, you're always like...

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.208
Like my posture gets better-
- Absolutely.

00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:17.041
- When a police officer arrives.

00:03:17.041 --> 00:03:17.874
- Or you pull off next to him at a light,

00:03:17.874 --> 00:03:18.707
and I'm like, "Everything's good, right?"

00:03:18.707 --> 00:03:20.860
- Yeah, and I'm like, "10 and
two, everything's going well.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.630
Here's my registration, just in case."

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:24.940
So I didn't ask to throw the siren on,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:25.960
but you do learn a lot.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.540
It's like just being
around, the way they move

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.030
and stuff like that is something
that's important to me.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.550
It's like the way they walk,
the way they handle themselves,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.620
the way they just do everything.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.730
It's second nature to them.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:36.840
- Yeah. Have y'all...

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.140
You've gotta see "61st Street".

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.126
I know y'all ain't seen
anything down there.

00:03:40.126 --> 00:03:41.640
(all laughing)
So.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:42.547
- You got us again.

00:03:42.547 --> 00:03:43.492
(Kelly mumbles)
(all laughing)

